(/** @param {any} G */ function (G) {
  "use strict";
  var R = G.LQ_DEPTH = G.LQ_DEPTH || { build: {}, text: { th: {}, en: {}, zh: {} } };
  var T = {"_title":"The dream in more depth",
    "sym.snake":"Books look at the snake's size, its colour and what it does: one gliding past is read lightly, one coiled around you speaks of closeness. Chinese books call it a \"little dragon\" and read hidden energy. Which matter are you hesitating to approach?",
    "sym.elephant":"A white elephant weighs differently from a grey one, and a calm elephant differently from one walking toward you. Thai books tie it to standing and stability; Chinese books play on xiang (elephant) sounding like xiang (good fortune), \"good omen\". What would you like to feel steady about?",
    "sym.dog":"The details are whether the dog is gentle or barking, whose it is, and whether you touch it; a dog nuzzling close reads as bonding. Chinese books see it as a loyal guardian of the home. Is there someone you would like to thank or stay closer to?",
    "sym.cat":"White, black or striped, soft or scratching, held in your arms or watched from afar: each changes the tone. Older Thai books stress independence, Chinese ones quiet watchfulness. Are you getting enough time that is truly your own?",
    "sym.fish":"Size, clear or muddy water, swimming freely or caught by you: these shift the reading. Thai books tie fish to plenty, while Chinese ones pun on yu (fish) and yu (surplus), \"surplus\". What in your life already feels like enough?",
    "sym.turtle":"A small or large turtle, plodding on or tucked into its shell, held or released: the books read each differently. Thai lore links it with patience and long life, Chinese lore with steady, auspicious endurance. What is worth taking slowly?",
    "sym.bird":"Which bird, what colour, singing or passing overhead, caged or free: the books weigh all of it. Thai readings stress news and freedom; Chinese ones like songbirds and magpies as carriers of cheerful word. What would you like to let lighten?",
    "sym.butterfly":"One butterfly or many, which colours, landing on you or drifting past: all noted. Thai books read a graceful change of stage; Chinese ones recall Zhuangzi's dream of being a butterfly and wondering who the dreamer is. Who are you becoming next?",
    "sym.horse":"White or dark, galloping or walking, ridden by you or only watched: the books look at the horse's pace and your own. Thai readings suggest drive and progress, and Chinese has a saying about success on the horse's arrival. Can you move at a pace that is yours?",
    "sym.cow":"A sleek or lean animal, in a paddy or a pen, walking toward you or turning away: these count. Thai books tie the buffalo to the home village and diligence, Chinese ones to honest hard work. Have you let your own effort rest lately?",
    "sym.pig":"A plump or thin pig, clean or muddy, penned or roaming, each shifts the reading. Thai books see household abundance; Chinese lore notes that the written character for home shows a pig under a roof. What at home makes you feel content?",
    "sym.chicken":"A rooster or hen, crowing at dawn or fighting, with chicks trailing behind or not: all part of the reading. Thai books see early-rising diligence, Chinese ones pun on ji (chicken) and ji (fortunate), \"fortunate\". What kind of morning would you like to build for yourself?",
    "sym.tiger":"A tiger sitting calmly, pacing round or lunging reads very differently, and so does watching versus stroking it. Thai books see authority or a protective force; Chinese ones call the tiger king of the mountain. Which kind of courage suits you best?",
    "sym.monkey":"A monkey playing in a tree, snatching something, or sitting still is read differently. Thai books see wit and mischief; Chinese ones pun on hou (monkey) and hou, a noble rank. Is your playful side getting any time with things you enjoy?",
    "sym.mouse":"One mouse or a crowd, in the house or the kitchen, chased off or left to pass: all counted. Thai books see small things piling up bit by bit; Chinese ones honour the mouse as the first zodiac animal, clever at finding a way. Which small matter deserves a gentle tidy-up?",
    "sym.crocodile":{"t":"A crocodile lying still by the water, swimming nearer or kept in a pond is read differently. Thai books link it with powerful people or patrons; older Chinese books say little of it and group it with other water creatures. Take care to treat influential people with calm respect and clear boundaries.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.naga":"Many heads or one, gold or green, in the water or in the sky: the books note it all. Thai lore holds the naga in special reverence as guardian of rivers and the faith; Chinese lore sits close to the dragon, tied to rain and authority. What do you honestly hold in respect?",
    "sym.bee":"A swarm or a lone bee, a hive close by or insects passing, honey gathered or a sting felt: all matter. Thai books read the busy cooperation of a group; Chinese ones play on feng (bee) and feng, being granted a rank. Who on your team deserves a word of thanks?",
    "sym.ant":"A long line or a lone ant, carrying food or crawling over you: the books note it all. Thai readings see small steady effort adding up; Chinese ones highlight the colony's teamwork. Which thing have you been doing one small step at a time that deserves a little credit?",
    "sym.frog":"A frog croaking loudly or sitting still, by the pond or hopping indoors: the details count. Thai lore links frogs to the calling of rain and fresh life after drought, and Chinese lore has the three-legged toad with a coin in its mouth. What kind of rain are you waiting for?",
    "sym.spider":"A spider spinning, dropping toward you, or its web being brushed away: the books use these details. Thai readings see patience in building one thread at a time; Chinese lore calls the small house spider a bringer of gladness. What are you quietly weaving?",
    "sym.crab":"A big crab or prawn, claws gripping, in the water or on a plate: the books note these. Thai readings see the claw as holding tight to what you have; Chinese ones pun on xie (crab) and xie, harmony. Is there something you are gripping so hard that it tires you?",
    "sym.egg":"One egg or many, whole, cracked or hatching, tilts the reading. Thai books see potential not yet revealed; Chinese ones treat the egg as a beginning that needs careful holding. Which plan is still an egg and needs a little warmth from you?",
    "sym.rain":"A light drizzle, a heavy downpour, or standing in it without shelter: each is read differently. Thai belief sees moisture and the start of the planting season; Chinese books see rain that comes on time as nourishment. Who or what does your heart want nourishing from?",
    "sym.thunder":"Thunder far or near, with lightning or without, startling you or leaving you still: the books weigh this. Thai belief reads a piece of news or a call to wake up; Chinese lore links it with the spring awakening when thunder rouses sleeping creatures. What is your mind calling you to look at?",
    "sym.rainbow":"A full arc or a half, vivid or faint, watched from afar or walked toward: the books note all of it. Thai belief reads it as a sign after a storm has passed; Chinese lore calls it a bridge between sky and earth. What heavy stretch has passed that your mind may now ease from?",
    "sym.sun":"Sunrise or sunset, gentle light or glare, warmth on the skin or squinting: the books use each detail. Thai belief reads honour and vitality; Chinese lore calls it the greatest yang, shining clarity. Where would you like things brought into the light, gently?",
    "sym.moon":"A full moon, a crescent or one behind cloud, with or without its reflection on water: the books note each. Thai belief reads coolness of heart and tenderness; Chinese lore links the moon to the mid-autumn reunion of families. Who would you like to sit and watch it with?",
    "sym.star":"One bright star or a whole sky, a shooting star, or one you can reach for: each changes the tone. Thai belief reads a guiding hope; Chinese lore links stars with choosing good times. Which small hope would you like to keep shining?",
    "sym.sea":"A calm sea or a choppy one, clear or cloudy, you on the shore or out in the water: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees breadth of opportunity and heart; Chinese sayings praise a sea that takes in every river. How much room does your heart have for something new?",
    "sym.river":"A slow river or a swift one, one you cross or one you sit beside: these are the details. Thai belief ties rivers to the course of life and the home village; Chinese sayings see flowing water as passing time. Do you want to go with the current or choose a heading yourself?",
    "sym.flood":{"t":"Water rising slowly to the knees or filling the house, and whether you can float: the books note it. Thai readings take it as feelings or tasks overflowing; Chinese ones see water as plenty that is hard to manage at once. Slow your pace, split things into small parts, and hold big decisions until you feel less swamped.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.mountain":"A mountain you climb step by step, watch from far away, or have already summited reads differently each time. Thai belief sees steadiness and a big goal; Chinese lore treats the mountain as something to lean on. Where are you on the climb, and where may you rest?",
    "sym.tree":"A broad shady tree, a sapling you just planted, or one you climb: each detail shifts the reading. Thai belief sees family roots and shelter; Chinese lore sees growth gathered year by year. Where have your own roots reached so far?",
    "sym.flower":"Which flower, what colour, blooming or beginning to fade, and whether you give or receive it: the books weigh all of this. Thai belief links flowers with goodwill and love, with the lotus carrying a spiritual meaning; Chinese lore ties each bloom to a season and a virtue. Who deserves a flower from you today?",
    "sym.fruit":"Ripe or green, sweet or sour, picked by you or handed over: the books look closely. Thai belief reads ripe fruit as the result of effort whose time has come; Chinese lore favours oranges and peaches as auspicious, sweet signs. What deserves to ripen in its own time?",
    "sym.fire":{"t":"A warm little fire, a candle flame or a spreading blaze, and whether you tended it or stepped back: the books use these. Thai belief reads rising energy or temper; Chinese lore calls fire a flourishing to handle with skill. Pause before speaking or messaging when feelings run high, and keep ordinary home safety habits.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.snow":"Light flakes drifting, a whole town blanketed, or snow you touch with bare hands: each shifts the reading. Thai books, from a land without snow, take it as an image of hush and purity; Chinese lore sees early snow as a promise of a good harvest. In which corner of life would you like a little quiet?",
    "sym.wind":{"t":"A cool breeze, a gust strong enough to make you hold on, or wind carrying things away: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees news or a change of direction; Chinese lore speaks of feng shui, wind and water as forces of a place. Hold what matters steady and decide when your mind has settled.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.cave":"A dark cave or one with light slanting in, entered alone or with company, and the way you came out: these are the details. Thai belief ties caves to meditation and seclusion; Chinese tales speak of hermits who retreat into caves. What would you like quiet time to think over?",
    "sym.wedding":"A wedding where you are bride or groom, or only a guest, merry or hushed, with someone offering blessings: the books look closely. Thai belief reads a commitment or the joining of two things; Chinese lore stresses harmony between two families. It does not say a real wedding is coming. What would you gladly commit to?",
    "sym.baby":"A baby asleep, crying or smiling at you, held in your arms or only watched: these are the details. Thai readings take it as a new project or idea needing care, not as news about having children; Chinese lore sees innocence. What needs gentle looking-after from you?",
    "sym.pregnant":"Feeling glad or uneasy about being pregnant, a large belly or a fresh discovery, yourself or someone else: the reading varies. Thai belief takes it as something forming inside you, such as a job or dream, and does not forecast children; Chinese lore stresses patient waiting. What is growing inside you?",
    "sym.ex":"The former partner smiling, greeting you or walking past, and whether it felt warm or heavy: the books differ. Thai belief sees a memory the heart still holds, with no advice to return or move on; Chinese lore says old faces appear when the mind wants to close a chapter. What would you thank or release from that time?",
    "sym.lover":"A lover close by, meeting your eyes, holding hands or appearing blurred, in a warm or quiet mood: the books note all. Thai belief reads a need for closeness and does not speak for the other person's feelings; Chinese lore links it to yin-yang balance. What more would you like in a relationship?",
    "sym.parents":"Parents smiling, talking or quiet, and you feeling close or far: the books pay attention. Thai belief ties it to gratitude and the debt of care; Chinese lore makes filial devotion the root of every virtue. Is there a short word you would like to say to them today?",
    "sym.elder":"A kindly elder offering advice, or simply sitting nearby: these are the details. Thai belief reads experience and seniors to lean on; Chinese lore respects the old as a storehouse of wisdom. On what matter would you value the view of someone who has walked further down the road?",
    "sym.ancestor":"A loved one who has passed, smiling, speaking softly or just near: the books read this gently. Thai belief links it with remembrance and dedicating merit; Chinese lore has honouring ancestors as an act of gratitude. It predicts nothing troubling. How would you like to remember them?",
    "sym.teacher":"A teacher advising warmly, giving a lesson or checking your work: these are the details. Thai belief honours the teacher as a giver of light; Chinese lore has the saying that one day as teacher, honoured as teacher for good. Which lesson is still waiting for you to put into practice?",
    "sym.monk":"A monk receiving alms with a smile, chanting or standing quietly, and whether you bow or make an offering: the books notice. Thai belief gives it weight as faith and calm; Chinese lore sees the renunciant as a symbol of letting go. What would you like to set down for a while to feel lighter?",
    "sym.friend":"A friend laughing with you, helping out or just sitting beside you: the books read each differently. Thai belief ties it to the good companion who steadies you; Chinese lore likens a true friend to a mirror of oneself. Is there a friend you would like to message with a simple \"I was thinking of you\"?",
    "sym.stranger":{"t":"A stranger smiling, asking directions or standing still, and whether you felt curious or wary: the books look closely. Thai belief reads an unfamiliar side of yourself or a new opening, judging no one as good or bad; Chinese lore sees a visitor bringing change. Meet what is new politely, with a sensible distance.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.celebrity":{"t":"A famous person you talk with, pass by or admire from afar: the books note this. Thai belief reads the figure as a mirror of qualities you admire in yourself; Chinese lore says reputation is the shadow of intention. Which quality of theirs would you like to practise?","c":true},
    "sym.child":"A child running about, crying or holding out a hand, and whether you know the child: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees a freshness the heart still keeps; Chinese lore sees the hope of a family. What did you love doing as a child that you have long set aside?",
    "sym.money":{"t":"Money received, counted or dropped, paper or coins: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees security and a sense of self-worth; Chinese lore says money is like water, coming and going along its channels. Keep income and spending written down, and read every money agreement fully before committing.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.gold":"Gold in a small piece or a pile, worn, stored, or handed to you: the books note it all. Thai belief regards gold as auspicious and confidence-giving; Chinese lore names it the metal element, the element of precision and clarity. Which of your own qualities deserves polishing and some pride?",
    "sym.ring":"Which finger it sits on, a snug fit or loose, or someone slipping it on you: the books look closely. Thai belief reads the ring as a symbol of bond and promise, not a hint of marriage; Chinese lore sees the circle as wholeness. Which promise to yourself would you like to revisit kindly?",
    "sym.gem":"A gem in red, green or clear, small or large, cut by your own hands or found by chance: these details count. Thai belief links gems with the nine-gem tradition and charm; Chinese lore honours jade as the stone of virtue. Which of your talents is still waiting to be polished?",
    "sym.key":"A key you hold, one that opens a door or that you cannot find, and what kind it is: the books look at all of it. Thai belief reads a way out or an opening that comes when you are ready; Chinese lore ties it to untying a knot through understanding. Which door in your life would you like to try now?",
    "sym.door":"A door standing open, firmly shut or just knocked on, with light or dark beyond: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees a chance or a change of life stage; Chinese lore treats doors as the entry of energy in feng shui. Which doorway are you ready to step through?",
    "sym.stairs":"Stairs going up or down, wide or narrow, climbed with ease or with effort: these are the details. Thai belief reads progress one step at a time; Chinese has a phrase about rising higher with each step. Is your next step small enough to start today?",
    "sym.car":{"t":"A car you drive yourself, ride in with others or find parked, on a clear road or a jam: the books look closely. Thai belief reads life's direction and responsibility; Chinese lore sees the wheel as control of your own pace. In real driving, stay attentive and rest when drowsy; the dream image is only a symbol.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.plane":"A plane lifting off smoothly, cruising in clear sky or waiting on the ground: the details shift the reading. Thai belief sees a leap toward a far goal; Chinese lore echoes the idea of rising above the clouds. How far are you ready to go, and what still needs preparing?",
    "sym.boat":"A small or large boat, drifting on still water or waves, rowed by you or steered by someone else: these are the details. Thai belief links boats to journeys and crossings; Chinese has a saying: in the same boat we help each other row. Who would you like to row alongside?",
    "sym.train":"A train running evenly, stopped at a station, or one you run to catch: the books note it. Thai belief reads a life plan with its timetable and rhythm; Chinese lore sees rails as a route already laid. Is your schedule right now too tight, or too loose?",
    "sym.mirror":{"t":"A mirror clear or fogged, your face distinct or blurred, and whether you smiled or stayed silent: the books note it all. Thai belief reads self-reflection, not a judgement of looks; Chinese lore treats the mirror as a lens on the heart. What would you say kindly to the person in the glass today?","c":true},
    "sym.phone":"A ringing phone, answered or left, with the voice clear or crackling: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees contact or news waiting; Chinese lore sees a bridge of voices between people. Is there someone you meant to call and have not yet?",
    "sym.letter":"A letter you read, write or leave sealed, and what colour the paper is: the books note this. Thai belief reads news or something in the heart not yet said; Chinese lore has the picture of a wild goose carrying a letter. If you wrote one, how would its first line begin?",
    "sym.book":"A book you can read clearly, text that swims, or a well-worn old volume: the books read each differently. Thai belief links books to wisdom and a chance to learn; Chinese has a saying: opening a book pays off. What subject would you like to study further?",
    "sym.shoes":"Shoes that fit well, pinch, go missing or are brand new: the books look closely. Thai belief reads readiness to step forward; Chinese lore sees footwear as support for each step along the path. Do you have what you need for your next step?",
    "sym.clothes":"New clothes that feel comfortable, snug or brightly coloured, chosen by you or given: these are the details. Thai belief reads a new role or image you are trying on; Chinese custom puts on new clothes to welcome the year. What new role would you like to wear comfortably?",
    "sym.bag":"A bag light, heavy or left open, and whether what is inside is complete or missing: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees what we carry along; Chinese lore ties it to preparing before a trip. What in your life's bag are you ready to put down?",
    "sym.clock":"A clock ticking normally, stopped, or running fast, and whether you glance at it anxiously or calmly: the books note it. Thai belief reads your sense of life's rhythm; Chinese has a word for the right moment. What matter deserves a little more time?",
    "sym.umbrella":"An umbrella held against rain, a broken or leaking one, and whether you shelter yourself or someone else: the books look at these. Thai belief reads support and generosity; Chinese lore sees the shade given by elders who protect. Whom would you like to shelter, and who might shelter you?",
    "sym.candle":"A candle burning steadily, flickering, or lit by you for someone else: the books use this image. Thai belief ties it to worship and the light of wisdom in rites; Chinese verse likens the candle that burns itself to give light to a giver. Have you given some of that time to yourself too?",
    "sym.buddha":"A seated Buddha image, a face with a kind smile, or a large figure you bow to: the books read with respect. In Thailand it stands for faith, mindfulness and a calm heart; Chinese Buddhist tradition sees compassion. In which part of today would you like extra mindfulness?",
    "sym.food":"A favourite dish shared with family, something you cook yourself, or a table full of plates: these are the details. Thai belief reads contentment and sharing; Chinese lore values the shared table as the gathering place of a family. Who would you like to sit down to a meal with soon?",
    "sym.house":"A spacious house, a cramped one, or one with a room you have just found: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees an inner space and the warmth of family; Chinese lore uses the house for stable footing and the feng shui of a life. Which room within you has not yet been opened?",
    "sym.temple":"A quiet temple, a crowded one, or one you walk into alone, with bells or chanting in the air: the books note the setting. Thai belief reads a need for calm and an inner refuge; Chinese lore sees the temple as a rest for the heart from bustle. When could you give yourself a quiet pause?",
    "sym.school":"A school familiar or strange, you as pupil or teacher, enjoying it or feeling pressed: these details count. Thai belief reads life lessons still being learned; Chinese lore treats learning as a lifelong path. What is life teaching you at the moment?",
    "sym.market":"A busy market, a quiet one, or you strolling and choosing at ease: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees a place of meeting people and exchanging; Chinese lore sees the heart of a community where everything circulates. What would you like to trade, whether knowledge or kindness?",
    "sym.office":"A tidy office or a cluttered one, colleagues smiling or busy, and you feeling confident or uneasy: these are the details. Thai belief reads your thoughts about your own role at work; Chinese lore sees a field for practising perseverance. Which part of your work makes you proudest?",
    "sym.bridge":"A solid bridge, one that sways a little, or one you cross step by step: the books note it. Thai belief reads crossing a stage of life or linking a relationship; Chinese lore sees a bridge joining two shores. What or whom would you like to reconnect with?",
    "sym.road":"A long straight road or a winding one, lit, forked or unfamiliar, walked alone or with company: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees the path of a life; Chinese has a saying: the road comes from walking it. Which fork would you take when you feel ready?",
    "sym.hotel":"A clean, convenient hotel, or one where you cannot find your room, staying alone or with family: the books use these. Thai belief reads a pause along life's path; Chinese lore sees a temporary rest on a journey. Is your life in a resting stretch or a moving one?",
    "sym.bathroom":"A clean bathroom, a messy one, or one lacking privacy, leaving you at ease or uncomfortable: the books note it all. Thai belief reads washing away feelings and releasing what has built up; Chinese lore sees cleansing water as a fresh start. What would you like to rinse out of your mind?",
    "sym.fly":"Flying low over the ground, high above a city, or slowing as you go: the books note these. Thai belief reads freedom and a wish to slip free of limits; Chinese Taoist tales speak of a spirit roaming the sky. What makes you feel lightest and most open?",
    "sym.fall":{"t":"Falling slowly, waking just as you drop, or a hand reaching to catch you: the books use these, without forecasting anything real. Thai belief reads a sense of lacking footing; Chinese lore sees a mind readjusting its balance. Ease your pace, tidy open matters one by one, and ask what helps you feel steadier.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.swim":"Swimming with ease, against the current, or just floating, in clear or cloudy water: the books read each differently. Thai belief reads your ability to stay with your own feelings; Chinese has a saying about doing something fluently with energy to spare. Where do you feel fluent now, and what else would you like to practise?",
    "sym.run":"Running joyfully, running to make it in time, or running while your legs will not move: the books give each its own meaning. Thai belief reads the energy to push yourself to a goal; Chinese lore sees a stride that needs breathing space. What are you running toward, and are you leaving yourself room to breathe?",
    "sym.chased":"Being followed by something clear or a vague shadow, getting away or turning to face it: the books note this, forecasting nothing real. Thai belief reads something the mind wishes to avoid; Chinese lore says such dreams come when tasks hang unfinished in thought. Which matter might feel lighter if you glanced at it?",
    "sym.lost":{"t":"Lost in a city, a forest or a tangled building, with or without someone to point the way: these details count. Thai belief reads not yet finding your direction; Chinese lore sees a time to stop and check the map of the heart. Pause before big decisions, and ask someone you trust to look at the way with you.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.exam":"Sitting an exam fully prepared, forgetting something, or running short of time, and what the paper is about: the books note it all. Thai belief reads feeling assessed and wanting to do well; Chinese has a saying about a sword ground for ten years, readiness built over time. In what area are you readier than you think?",
    "sym.cry":"Crying quietly, sobbing hard, or being comforted by someone beside you, and waking lighter or heavier: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees release of feelings held in; Chinese lore holds that tears wash the heart. If it lingers in your mind, talking with someone you trust may help.",
    "sym.laugh":"Laughing loudly with friends, laughing alone, or waking mid-laugh, and what set it off: the books note this. Thai belief reads ease of heart and goodwill; Chinese has a saying about a mouth often open in laughter, a refreshed life. What has truly made you smile lately?",
    "sym.dance":"Dancing alone, with a partner or in a crowd, at a quick tempo or a slow one: the books give each its own meaning. Thai belief reads free expression of feeling; Chinese lore sees dance as a celebratory rite. What rhythm would you like to move to today?",
    "sym.travel":"Travelling to somewhere new, returning to somewhere old or waiting to board, with or without a companion: these are the details. Thai belief reads a wish for a change of scene; Chinese has a saying: read ten thousand books, walk ten thousand miles. Where does your heart want to visit?",
    "sym.move":"Moving to a roomier new home, arriving with half your things, or still packing: the books read each differently. Thai belief sees a change of life stage and releasing the old; Chinese custom picks a day for entering a new home. What are you ready to take with you, and what would you leave behind?",
    "sym.merit":"Making merit yourself, offering alms, helping others or joining a merit event with family: the books note the form. Thai belief reads the ease of heart that comes from giving and thinking of others; Chinese lore stresses gathering small kindnesses steadily. Which kind of giving warms you most?",
    "sym.argue":{"t":"Arguing with someone you know or a stranger, loudly or in silence, and whether it ended in making up: the books differ. Thai belief sees a difference of view the heart is holding; Chinese lore sees a rift best untied with courteous words. Take a breath before raising the real matter, and choose a calm moment.",
    "c":true},
    "sym.win":"Receiving a prize on stage, winning a small draw, or being praised, with applause or without: these are the details. Thai belief reads wanting your effort to be seen; Chinese has a saying: real worth earns its name. Which of your efforts deserves a compliment?",
    "sym.teeth":"Teeth loosening without pain, several at once, or a firm tooth wobbling: older books tie this to family and age, while this reading keeps to change and worry about self-image or the words you use, not a forecast. Is there something about how you communicate that is weighing on you?",
    "sym.hair":"A short cut, a new colour, or hair growing quickly longer, and whether you felt pleased or a little sorry: the books note it all. Thai belief reads shedding a burden and a fresh start; Chinese lore plays on fa (hair) sounding like fa (to flourish). What small change might help you feel fresh?",
    "sym.hands":"Hands clean or soiled, a hand that holds yours, or hands that seem to be more than two: the books use these details. Thai belief reads the ability to act and to help; Chinese lore sees hands as tools of diligence and goodwill. What would these hands like to do for someone you care about?",
    "time.early":"Thai books divide the night into watches, and a dream from the first watch, early in the evening, carries the least weight, since the mind is still circling the day just gone. It reads best as a reflection of what you thought and felt today rather than a signal about what is ahead.",
    "time.late":"A dream between midnight and about three belongs to the middle watch, and the books give it moderate weight. It falls in deep sleep, when we rarely recall dreams, so one that stays with you is taken to concern something the mind has held for a while. Treat it as food for thought, not a verdict.",
    "time.dawn":"A dream from about three until first light belongs to the last watch, and Thai books give it the most weight, partly because it is vividly remembered and still close when you wake. That weight is a belief, not a promise; it is simply a reason to pay closer attention to what the images reflect for you.",
    "time.nap":"Thai books give a daytime nap dream only light weight, since sleep is shallow and sounds of the room seep in. The images tend to blend with whatever was still on your mind that day, so they serve best as a small question to yourself rather than an indicator of anything.",
    "mood.happy":"Waking up happy softens how a dream's images seem, and we tend to remember the pleasant parts more sharply. Thai books hold that an easy heart tilts the reading toward the constructive. Notice which person or place in the dream carried that joy, and look for a chance to bring a little of it into the day.",
    "mood.excited":{"t":"Excitement on waking suggests the dream still has energy for you and that part of you wants to get moving on something. The books read it as enthusiasm rather than a signal of what is to come. Jot your thoughts down first, then look again once the buzz settles, before acting on them.",
    "c":true},
    "mood.calm":"A calm waking lets you see the dream's images as they are, with less distortion from feeling. Thai books hold that a still mind reads meaning most closely, which makes this a good moment to hear the quiet voice in the dream and jot down whatever you still remember before it fades.",
    "mood.anxious":"Feeling anxious on waking is usually the mind sorting through things left open, not an indicator of what will happen. Frightening images are read as worries being digested. Writing each concern down can bring clarity, and telling someone you trust is one option that may help it feel lighter.",
    "mood.sad":"Sadness on waking can mean the heart let something go during sleep. The books read it as missing someone or wanting care, not as a sign that anything difficult is coming. Give yourself a few minutes, drink some water, open a window, and if it feels heavy, talking with someone you trust is another option that may help.",
    "mood.confused":"Confusion on waking often comes from images that do not join into a story, which Thai books treat as ordinary for dreams. You need not explain every scene. Pick the single image that clings to you most, consider that, and leave the rest for now; clarity may come on its own, a little later."};
  R.text.en.dream = T;
})(typeof globalThis !== "undefined" ? globalThis : this);
/* END depth en.dream */
